"note": "Duplicate groups and anchor records inside the Lane A pool. Not cheaply regenerable: the four-way classification is judgement, not a rule. Identifiers are Semantic Scholar paper ids, except OpenCitations-only works which carry no S2 id and appear as oc:<doi>. In every group the first entry is the survivor under the published-beats-preprint rule; the rest are listed in duplicate_losers and should be retired, not deleted, so the merge stays auditable. Pool arithmetic: 2,061 records, minus 15 anchors, minus 93 losers, leaves 1,953 curatable works.",
"counts": {"garbage": 5, "acm_twin": 23, "preprint_pub": 31, "other": 32},

"kind_notes": {
"garbage": "MUST NEVER BE MERGED. Semantic Scholar sometimes stores a USENIX cover page as a paper's title, so several unrelated papers all read 'This paper is included in the Proceedings of the Nth USENIX Symposium...'. These groups are artefacts of title matching; the papers are distinct and their real titles need recovering from their DOIs.",
"acm_twin": "ACM deposits the same paper under both a proceedings DOI and an ACM SIGPLAN Notices DOI, so the same work appears twice with different DOIs. This is the same defect that affects the anchors themselves. 22 of the 23 pair an OpenCitations-only record with a Semantic Scholar one, which is why the OpenCitations net gain is about 201 rather than 223.",
"preprint_pub": "arXiv or technical-report version alongside the published one. TVM is the extreme case: 2,117 citations on the preprint against 356 on the OSDI paper.",
"other": "Same work under two records for reasons that are neither an ACM twin nor a preprint pair -- book chapters reissued, journal extensions, and records differing only in punctuation."
},

